      "text": "First, we need to determine how far the train travels while the front of the train is entering the tunnel. Since the train is traveling at 60 miles per hour, we can find the time it takes for the front of the train to enter the tunnel.\n\nTime taken to enter the tunnel = Distance / Speed\nTime taken to enter the tunnel = 1 mile / 60 miles per hour\nTime taken to enter the tunnel = 1/60 hours\n\nNext, we need to convert this time to minutes, as the problem states that the train takes 3 minutes to completely clear the tunnel.\n\nTime taken to enter the tunnel in minutes = (1/60) hours * 60 minutes per hour\nTime taken to enter the tunnel in minutes = 1 minute\n\nNow that we know the time it takes for the front of the train to enter the tunnel, we can determine the length of the tunnel. Since the train travels for 1 minute while entering the tunnel and the front of the train exits 3 minutes after the front of the train entered, the train has been traveling for a total of 3 minutes.\n\nTotal distance traveled by the train = Speed * Time\nTotal distance traveled by the train = 60 miles per hour * 3 minutes\n\nTo convert the time to hours, we divide by 60 minutes per hour.\n\nTime in hours = 3 minutes / 60 minutes per hour\nTime in hours = 1/20 hours\n\nNow we can find the total distance traveled by the train.\n\nTotal distance traveled by the train = 60 miles per hour * 1/20 hours\nTotal distance traveled by the train = 3 miles\n\nSince the train travels 3 miles while entering the tunnel and the tunnel is 1 mile long, the length of the tunnel must be:\n\nLength of the tunnel = Total distance traveled - Distance traveled by the front of the train\nLength of the tunnel = 3 miles - 1 mile\nLength of the tunnel = 2 miles\n\nTherefore, the length of the tunnel is \\boxed{2} miles.",
